{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And this is the gate of heaven.</b> Scripture mentions the word זה three times. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: He showed him the Temple built, and destroyed, and built. Built — 'How awesome is this place'; destroyed — 'this is none other'; and built — 'and this is the gate of heaven.' According to this, Scripture alludes to only two Temples." "<b>And it is possible</b> to interpret that there is in Scripture an allusion to three Temples, from what is well known: that the word זה alludes to the Shekhinah, as it is said (Exodus 15:2): ״זה אלי ואנוהו״. And he said, 'How awesome is this place' — concerning the First Temple, in which the Shekhinah dwelt. 'This is none other' — concerning the Second Temple, in which the Shekhinah did not dwell, as it is written in the prophecy of Haggai (Haggai 1:8): ״וארצה בו ואכבדה״. And אכבד is written (defectively), for the Shekhinah, which is the letter ה, was absent from there. 'And this is the gate of heaven' — concerning the Third Temple, which will be built speedily in our days, when the service and the Shekhinah will return to Jerusalem as in days of old. And therefore here he mentioned 'heaven,' because it will be the work of heaven, not a building of men as the earlier ones, concerning which he mentioned 'place' and 'house.' And so Moses alluded in the blessings of Benjamin, in whose portion the Temple lay: that which he said (Deuteronomy 33:12) ״ישכון לבטח עליו״ — this is the First Temple, in which the Shekhinah dwelt, which is called ידיד ה׳. ״חופף עליו כל היום״ — this is the Second Temple, in which the Shekhinah did not dwell. ״ובין כתפיו שכן״ — this is the Third Temple, destined to be, in which the Shekhinah will dwell. And so you will find in tractate Nazir: 'היכל ה׳ היכל ה׳ היכל ה׳ המה' — this is the First Temple, and the Second Temple, and the Third Temple; from this we learn: two were destroyed, and three shall stand forever — end of quotation." "<b>And it appears</b> that he mentioned in the third instance the word המה, which is a term indicating an eternal and enduring thing, as in the language of (Ezekiel 10:20): ״ואדע כי כרובים המה״. And similarly (Psalms 25:6): ״כי מעולם המה״. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded on the language of (Exodus 27:20): ״כתית למאור״. The First Temple stood 410 years and the Second Temple 420, and both together equal the numerical value of כתית; and this matter is alluded to in the expression כתית, because the two Temples were ground down and both were destroyed; but the Third Temple is להעלות נר תמיד — to raise up a perpetual lamp — that is, its elevation is perpetual and its light shall never cease." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'And this is the gate of heaven' — from here you learn that one who prays in Jerusalem is as if he prays before the Throne of Glory, for the gate of heaven is open to receive the prayers of Israel, as it is said: 'And this is the gate of heaven.' And the intention is that it is the gate of heaven to ascend from there the prayers and the offerings."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:56.092698583Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}